1: # P3F.32. All sphere orders of the positive-diagonal OS49 filling
2: 
3: **Proved.** Use the positive-diagonal fan of P3F.31, the cap
4: order \(m=3k\), \(k>0\), and the vector
5: \[
6:  \ell=n(1,-1,0,0)^t+d\delta,\qquad n\in\{1,-1\},\quad d\in\mathbb Z.
7: \]
8: The exact construction below extends the positive-fan page and
9: angular origins to every such order and twist. Every resulting
10: sphere family has zero positive-loop return index and a product
11: mapping torus. P3K.17 proves the same return for the
12: negative-diagonal filling by the explicit charged flop bordism.
13: 
14: ## 1. The actual closing map and its origins
15: 
16: Put \(\lambda=r^m\), \(v=\log r/(2\pi i)\) and
17: \(s=\log q/(2\pi i)\). The periods are
18: \[
19:  u=(2s,s)^t+U(q),\qquad
20:  \gamma=(-3s,-s+mv)^t+C(q),\qquad D=3U+2C,
21: \]
22: so
23: \[
24:  H=-u-\gamma,\qquad K=3u+2\gamma=D+(s+2mv)\delta.
25: \]
26: The invariant cap covector evaluates to \(n\) on \(\ell\).
27: When \(v\) increases by one, its affine logarithmic origin changes
28: by \(n\log t\,\delta/(2\pi i)\). The fixed extra displacement
29: \(d\delta/m\) has no parameter difference. Consequently
30: multiplication of the ordinary line coordinate by \(t^n\) closes
31: the family. It is a unit at the type-II value and is \(q^{-n}\)
32: at the cusp. Its toric map is the integral vertical translation
33: by \(n\), which preserves the positive-diagonal fan.
34: 
35: On \(q=Q^{2m}\), choose the two extending origins
36: \[
37:  \begin{aligned}
38:  \sigma(s,v)&=nvD+nmv(v-1)\delta,\\
39:  o(s,v)&=-\frac{nsD}{2m}
40:           -\frac{ns^2}{4m}\delta-nsv\delta.
41:  \end{aligned}
42: \]
43: The first is bounded in the cusp charts; the second has parameter
44: difference \(-ns\delta\), exactly the logarithm of the closing
45: map at the cusp. Their difference is
46: \[
47:  F(s,v)=\frac{nD(s+2mv)}{2m}
48:        +\left(\frac{n(s+2mv)^2}{4m}-nmv\right)\delta,
49: \]
50: and direct subtraction gives
51: \[
52:  \begin{aligned}
53:  F(s,v+\theta)-F(s,v)
54:     &=n\theta K+nm\theta(\theta-1)\delta,\\
55:  F(s+2m,v)-F(s,v)&=nK+nm\delta.
56:  \end{aligned}
57: \]
58: The cyclic descent is therefore by an integral period. Its line
59: gauge has winding \(2mn\), while the displayed real angular gauge
60: has zero values at both endpoints. The actual positive \(r\)-loop
61: has \(n\) turns of the primitive \(K\)-circle. For \(n=-1\) the
62: parametrized rotating trace is reversed; the index changes sign
63: by P3F.11.
64: 
65: ## 2. The page, including the finite-cap line
66: 
67: The finite cap is the actual free affine quotient of P3F.29.
68: Its gamma displacement is \(n/m\). For \(n=\pm1\) its circle
69: base is the weighted Hopf three-sphere with one exceptional orbit
70: of order \(m\), with the gamma orientation reversed in the
71: negative case. The regular-binding page maps to the complex
72: elliptic base with degree \(m\), with full branching at the
73: finite logarithmic value and at the regular binding value. It is
74: the explicit cyclic cover obtained by taking an \(m\)-th root
75: of a rational coordinate whose zero and pole are those two
76: values. The genus of its compact base is zero.
77: 
78: Here the cap hypothesis is verified by the actual root charts:
79: its linear part has order three and fixes \(\ell\), and its
80: \(j\)-th power has gamma displacement \(jn/m\). For
81: \(0<j<m\) this displacement is nonintegral because
82: \(\gcd(n,m)=1\). It is therefore free. No nonidentity
83: delta rotation compensates such a displacement. Thus the
84: delta action is free on these cap charts as well, exactly
85: as required in the trace construction.
86: 
87: The finite-value root is a multiple of three, so the supplied
88: order-three good reduction extends over that root as a smooth
89: elliptic surface. The line on this root is the pulled-back
90: degree-zero section line. The extra \(d\delta/m\) in the affine
91: deck multiplies its circle coordinate by a constant root of
92: unity. It acts as the identity on the elliptic source coordinate.
93: Thus its change of gluing is a scalar transition on the base:
94: on a cut annulus it is the constant character
95: \(\exp(2\pi i d/m)\). After the page has gone around all \(m\)
96: sheets its total character is \(\exp(2\pi i d)=1\).
97: Choosing the scalar transitions on the individual sheet overlaps
98: gives a line pulled back from the base page. This describes the
99: actual additional gauge; it does not change the Picard class on
100: an elliptic fibre. A different cap trivialization can change the
101: compact extension only by an integral fibre class.
102: 
103: At the cusp choose \(m\) norm shells in the actual \(M^\times\)
104: subfan, with radii \(R_1<\cdots<R_m\) satisfying \(cR_m<R_1\).
105: The exact height defects and no-reentry proof in P3F.31 apply to
106: every one of these shells. Its explicit positive-multiplier
107: annular lift glues them to the \(m\) outer page sheets. At the
108: type-II values use the original line-complement map. This is an
109: embedded page \(V_m\subset Y\setminus F\) carrying the actual
110: delta line \(M\), up to the base line just described.
111: 
112: Its regular compact cap has \(m\) type-II and \(m\) type-\(I_2\)
113: fibres. The section and zero section remain disjoint under the
114: base cover and its good-reduction extension. More explicitly,
115: the degree-\(3k\) rational coordinate power factors through
116: the degree-three cover of P3F.31; the further degree-\(k\)
117: cover branches only at its smooth good-reduction fibre and
118: at the regular binding fibre. It pulls back two already
119: disjoint sections, so it introduces no section intersection.
120: Hence
121: \[
122:  \chi(\mathcal O)=k,\qquad \sigma=-8k,\qquad
123:  M_{\mathrm{rel}}^2=-2k,\qquad p_{\mathrm{rel}}=-24k.
124: \]
125: These identities are unaffected by an integral fibre-class
126: change: the section line has degree zero on a fibre, and the
127: fibre has square zero.
128: 
129: ## 3. The actual graph line and return
130: 
131: Use the \(K\)-compression and its exact inverse boundary angle.
132: The annular lift above leaves the binding base coordinate
133: unchanged. The boundary phase now winds \(m\) times, so the
134: normal pushoff equation of P3F.31 confines its intersections
135: with the unrotated compression to \(m\) proper boundary arcs.
136: The page misses the fixed sphere through its actual line subfan;
137: therefore all remaining intersections are over a compact regular
138: base interval. Enlarge the \(m\) lifts to disjoint half-disc
139: bands. Each pair is
140: \[
141:  (T^2\times D_i,\ T^2\times A_i),
142:  \qquad H^2(T^2\times D_i,T^2\times A_i;\mathbb Z)=0.
143: \]
144: The exact radial homotopy on the complement, followed by this
145: restriction injectivity, identifies the graph-normal meridian
146: line with the actual page line on all of \(V_m\).
147: 
148: The cap marking still has
149: \((u,\gamma)=(2H+K,-3H-K)\). Its determinant is one, and its
150: vanishing primitive cycle is \(w\). The two marked caps and
151: their line restrictions are precisely those in P3F.31. Thus
152: the actual sphere-bundle bordism has
153: \[
154:  b^2=-8k,\qquad p_1(T\bar X)=-24k,
155: \]
156: and one primitive \(K\)-turn has
157: \[
158:  A_K=\frac{3(-8k)-(-24k)}{24}=0.
159: \]
160: The explicitly computed positive parameter loop has \(n\)
161: turns and the endpoint-zero angular delta gauge, so its index
162: is \(nA_K=0\). The topology of the positive-fan filling follows
163: from P3F.29 and the actual common blowup of P3F.31, which adds
164: the same primitive cohomology summands to both fillings. It is
165: the standard smooth six-sphere. The Hopf transfer P3F.22 gives
166: oriented return class zero, and P3F.24 gives the product mapping
167: torus for every \(k,d\) and both signs of \(n\).
